course: dictionary
lesson: 6160
title: Finding the right meaning in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «A food processor or mincer has a narrow feed tube on purpose, so a hand cannot fit.» The words food, feed tube and pusher show that this is a kitchen machine.
explain: A mincer is a thing in this lesson. In the UK, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland, a mincer pushes meat or other food through a plate with holes, so it comes out in very small pieces. In the US this is usually called a «meat grinder».
explain: Always ask: which meaning fits the clues? Try it in the sentence. If you read «put the meat through the mincer», the meat is the food and the mincer is the machine, so the kitchen meaning fits.
fact: A mincer is a kitchen machine that cuts food into very small pieces by pushing it through a plate with holes.
fact: A mincer turns beef into mince, which is used for burgers, shepherd's pie and spaghetti bolognese.
fact: Old hand mincers had a crank handle and were clamped to the table.
fact: Safety rule: never put fingers into a mincer. Always use the pusher, because the narrow feed tube is designed to keep hands out.
fact: An old, informal, and rare meaning of mincer is a person who walks with small, fussy steps. This use can be unkind, so be careful with it.
exercise: «Put the meat through the mincer.» What is the mincer here? | *A kitchen machine that minces food | A butcher's shop | A person who walks with small, fussy steps | A type of hamburger bun
exercise: In the US, what is a mincer usually called? | A food processor | *A meat grinder | A pepper mill | A cheese slicer
exercise: Why does a mincer have a narrow feed tube? | So the mince can be sold in bags | *So a hand cannot fit inside, keeping fingers safe | To make the mince taste sweeter | To make the food go faster
test: Which food goes into a mincer to make mince for burgers? | *Beef | Bread | Rice | Apples
test: What should you use to push food into a mincer? | Your fingers | A metal fork | *The pusher | Your elbow
exam: Jayden reads, «The old mincer shuffled across the room in tiny, fussy steps.» Which meaning fits? | *A person who walks with small, fussy steps | A type of coat | A shop that sells sausages | A kitchen machine that minces meat
